{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "unitId": "chapter-when-moral-seriousness-scales-parts-part-4-what-remains-possible-chapter-9-rebuilding-density",
  "generationHash": "sha256:51fffafb0ca1dae583c0e67a460c186c93473cdf5054ab0d9921c58a58481a8e",
  "granularity": "segment-only",
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": "s0001",
      "text": "Chapter 9: Rebuilding Density",
      "startMs": 0.0,
      "endMs": 2298.0,
      "charStart": 0,
      "charEnd": 29
    },
    {
      "id": "s0002",
      "text": "Rebuilding Density",
      "startMs": 2902.0,
      "endMs": 4075.0,
      "charStart": 30,
      "charEnd": 48
    },
    {
      "id": "s0003",
      "text": "Scale thins reciprocity, but it does not eliminate the possibility of rebuilding it.",
      "startMs": 4133.0,
      "endMs": 9415.0,
      "charStart": 50,
      "charEnd": 134
    },
    {
      "id": "s0004",
      "text": "What disappears at scale is not morality itself, but moral density—the concentration of visible consequence, reciprocal constraint, and corrigibility that stabilizes leadership at human scale.",
      "startMs": 9415.0,
      "endMs": 22639.0,
      "charStart": 134,
      "charEnd": 326
    },
    {
      "id": "s0005",
      "text": "The task, then, is not to romanticize smallness or deny scale.",
      "startMs": 23208.0,
      "endMs": 27886.0,
      "charStart": 328,
      "charEnd": 390
    },
    {
      "id": "s0006",
      "text": "It is to reintroduce density deliberately inside larger systems.",
      "startMs": 28130.0,
      "endMs": 32472.0,
      "charStart": 391,
      "charEnd": 455
    },
    {
      "id": "s0007",
      "text": "Scale cannot be reversed.",
      "startMs": 32646.0,
      "endMs": 34376.0,
      "charStart": 457,
      "charEnd": 482
    },
    {
      "id": "s0008",
      "text": "Density can be designed.",
      "startMs": 34550.0,
      "endMs": 36431.0,
      "charStart": 483,
      "charEnd": 507
    },
    {
      "id": "s0009",
      "text": "The first mechanism is local reciprocity within large systems.",
      "startMs": 36710.0,
      "endMs": 40878.0,
      "charStart": 509,
      "charEnd": 571
    },
    {
      "id": "s0010",
      "text": "Even in complex institutions, work occurs in bounded units—teams, committees, divisions, communities of practice.",
      "startMs": 40878.0,
      "endMs": 48865.0,
      "charStart": 571,
      "charEnd": 684
    },
    {
      "id": "s0011",
      "text": "Where consequences remain visible within these units, restraint becomes reciprocal again.",
      "startMs": 48865.0,
      "endMs": 53962.0,
      "charStart": 684,
      "charEnd": 773
    },
    {
      "id": "s0012",
      "text": "Actors encounter the human cost of decisions.",
      "startMs": 53962.0,
      "endMs": 56899.0,
      "charStart": 773,
      "charEnd": 818
    },
    {
      "id": "s0013",
      "text": "Feedback is shorter.",
      "startMs": 57491.0,
      "endMs": 58792.0,
      "charStart": 822,
      "charEnd": 842
    },
    {
      "id": "s0014",
      "text": "Correction is faster.",
      "startMs": 58966.0,
      "endMs": 60487.0,
      "charStart": 843,
      "charEnd": 864
    },
    {
      "id": "s0015",
      "text": "Large systems that preserve meaningful sub-units retain pockets of moral density where contestability can survive.",
      "startMs": 60487.0,
      "endMs": 68230.0,
      "charStart": 864,
      "charEnd": 978
    },
    {
      "id": "s0016",
      "text": "The second mechanism is distributed authority.",
      "startMs": 68602.0,
      "endMs": 71330.0,
      "charStart": 981,
      "charEnd": 1027
    },
    {
      "id": "s0017",
      "text": "Concentrated authority accelerates action but magnifies asymmetry.",
      "startMs": 71330.0,
      "endMs": 75719.0,
      "charStart": 1027,
      "charEnd": 1093
    },
    {
      "id": "s0018",
      "text": "Distributed authority slows coordination while increasing exposure to dissent.",
      "startMs": 75719.0,
      "endMs": 80804.0,
      "charStart": 1093,
      "charEnd": 1171
    },
    {
      "id": "s0019",
      "text": "When decision-making is shared across roles rather than centralized in office, legitimacy becomes less dependent on individual restraint and more dependent on process.",
      "startMs": 80804.0,
      "endMs": 90904.0,
      "charStart": 1171,
      "charEnd": 1338
    },
    {
      "id": "s0020",
      "text": "Distributed authority does not eliminate conflict.",
      "startMs": 90904.0,
      "endMs": 94236.0,
      "charStart": 1338,
      "charEnd": 1388
    },
    {
      "id": "s0021",
      "text": "It prevents unilateral normalization.",
      "startMs": 94898.0,
      "endMs": 97557.0,
      "charStart": 1393,
      "charEnd": 1430
    },
    {
      "id": "s0022",
      "text": "The third mechanism is designed friction.",
      "startMs": 97882.0,
      "endMs": 100866.0,
      "charStart": 1432,
      "charEnd": 1473
    },
    {
      "id": "s0023",
      "text": "Friction is often treated as inefficiency.",
      "startMs": 100866.0,
      "endMs": 103582.0,
      "charStart": 1473,
      "charEnd": 1515
    },
    {
      "id": "s0024",
      "text": "In moral systems, friction can be protective.",
      "startMs": 103931.0,
      "endMs": 106694.0,
      "charStart": 1517,
      "charEnd": 1562
    },
    {
      "id": "s0025",
      "text": "Mandatory review, sunset clauses, transparent reporting requirements, and procedural delays are forms of intentional resistance to automatic escalation.",
      "startMs": 106694.0,
      "endMs": 116865.0,
      "charStart": 1562,
      "charEnd": 1714
    },
    {
      "id": "s0026",
      "text": "They do not assume actors will choose restraint.",
      "startMs": 117224.0,
      "endMs": 119883.0,
      "charStart": 1716,
      "charEnd": 1764
    },
    {
      "id": "s0027",
      "text": "They require reconsideration before authority expands.",
      "startMs": 119883.0,
      "endMs": 123529.0,
      "charStart": 1764,
      "charEnd": 1818
    },
    {
      "id": "s0028",
      "text": "Finally, institutional limits preserve constraint across time.",
      "startMs": 123970.0,
      "endMs": 127975.0,
      "charStart": 1821,
      "charEnd": 1883
    },
    {
      "id": "s0029",
      "text": "Authority that expires must be renewed.",
      "startMs": 128300.0,
      "endMs": 131319.0,
      "charStart": 1884,
      "charEnd": 1923
    },
    {
      "id": "s0030",
      "text": "Roles that rotate prevent identity fusion.",
      "startMs": 131319.0,
      "endMs": 134338.0,
      "charStart": 1923,
      "charEnd": 1965
    },
    {
      "id": "s0031",
      "text": "Decisions that require periodic reauthorization prevent precedent from hardening silently into permission.",
      "startMs": 134338.0,
      "endMs": 141060.0,
      "charStart": 1965,
      "charEnd": 2071
    },
    {
      "id": "s0032",
      "text": "Where legitimacy must be re-earned rather than inherited indefinitely, harm cannot proceed without renewed justification.",
      "startMs": 141060.0,
      "endMs": 149187.0,
      "charStart": 2071,
      "charEnd": 2192
    },
    {
      "id": "s0033",
      "text": "None of these mechanisms guarantee moral seriousness.",
      "startMs": 150069.0,
      "endMs": 153355.0,
      "charStart": 2197,
      "charEnd": 2250
    },
    {
      "id": "s0034",
      "text": "They do not create virtue.",
      "startMs": 153482.0,
      "endMs": 155085.0,
      "charStart": 2252,
      "charEnd": 2278
    },
    {
      "id": "s0035",
      "text": "They create conditions in which virtue remains possible.",
      "startMs": 155085.0,
      "endMs": 158266.0,
      "charStart": 2278,
      "charEnd": 2334
    },
    {
      "id": "s0036",
      "text": "They reduce the distance between authority and consequence.",
      "startMs": 158266.0,
      "endMs": 161807.0,
      "charStart": 2334,
      "charEnd": 2393
    },
    {
      "id": "s0037",
      "text": "They make contestability durable rather than symbolic.",
      "startMs": 162225.0,
      "endMs": 165777.0,
      "charStart": 2396,
      "charEnd": 2450
    },
    {
      "id": "s0038",
      "text": "They interrupt momentum before it becomes default.",
      "startMs": 165777.0,
      "endMs": 168854.0,
      "charStart": 2450,
      "charEnd": 2500
    },
    {
      "id": "s0039",
      "text": "Rebuilding density is not an argument for paralysis.",
      "startMs": 169446.0,
      "endMs": 172627.0,
      "charStart": 2503,
      "charEnd": 2555
    },
    {
      "id": "s0040",
      "text": "It is an argument for bounded acceleration.",
      "startMs": 172627.0,
      "endMs": 175413.0,
      "charStart": 2555,
      "charEnd": 2598
    },
    {
      "id": "s0041",
      "text": "Systems that incorporate expiration, distribution, and friction move more slowly.",
      "startMs": 175413.0,
      "endMs": 181787.0,
      "charStart": 2598,
      "charEnd": 2679
    },
    {
      "id": "s0042",
      "text": "They also accumulate fewer automatic harms.",
      "startMs": 181787.0,
      "endMs": 184446.0,
      "charStart": 2679,
      "charEnd": 2722
    },
    {
      "id": "s0043",
      "text": "“Scale cannot be undone.",
      "startMs": 184910.0,
      "endMs": 186454.0,
      "charStart": 2727,
      "charEnd": 2751
    },
    {
      "id": "s0044",
      "text": "But constraint can be redesigned.”",
      "startMs": 186605.0,
      "endMs": 188544.0,
      "charStart": 2752,
      "charEnd": 2786
    }
  ]
}
